                "content": ".\n\n**Solution Overview**\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|S_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n* the \\(x\\)-axis  \\((y=\\text{const})\\);\n* the \\(y\\)-axis  \\((x=\\text{const})\\);\n* the line \\(x+y=0\\)  \\((x+y=\\text{const})\\).\n\nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\nWe have to determine, for each \\(n\\ge3\\), all integers \\(k\\ge0\\) for which there exist  \n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  Two easy constructions  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n  All \\(\\ell_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(\\,x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add a sunny line through the\n  remaining point \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr).\n  \\]\n\n  The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the line \\(L\\) covers\n  \\((n,1)\\).  Exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\nThus **\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  An impossibility lemma  \n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of distinct lines contain at most \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n> Then at least two columns (i.e. sets \\(\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge1\\}\\)) contain **no** vertical line of\n> \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) possible vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \nIf at most \\(n-2\\) of them belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\), at least two of the \\(n\\) columns are\nmissing a vertical line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Consequence for \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with **\\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines** exists.\nThen the number of non‑sunny lines is \\(n-k\\le n-2\\); by the lemma there are two columns\nwithout a vertical line.  Choose the two right‑most such columns, namely  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad   \nC_{n}   =\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause no vertical line of the configuration passes through these columns,\nevery point of \\(C_{n-1}\\cup C_{n}\\) must lie on a **non‑vertical** line\n(either horizontal, diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\), or sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The point \\((n,1)\\)\n\nA non‑vertical line that contains \\((n,1)\\) is one of the following:\n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* a sunny line (any other slope).\n\nIn each case the line meets column \\(C_{n-1}\\) in **exactly one** of its two points:\n\\(y=1\\) meets \\((n-1,1)\\), while \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\((n-1,2)\\); a sunny line meets at most\none point of a given column because two distinct points of the same column would force the\nline to be vertical, which it is not.\n\nHence the two points of \\(C_{n-1}\\) cannot be covered by the same line that contains\n\\((n,1)\\); they need **two further distinct non‑vertical lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Counting the required non‑vertical lines\n\nThus we need at least three distinct non‑vertical lines:\n\n* one line for \\((n,1)\\);\n* one line for \\((n-1,1)\\);\n* one line for \\((n-1,2)\\).\n\nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) lie in columns that *do* have a vertical line, so they are already\ncovered. Consequently the configuration must contain **at least three** non‑vertical lines.\n\nBut the total number of non‑vertical lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{all lines}}-\\underbrace{v}_{\\text{vertical lines}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(v\\) is the number of vertical lines present.  \nSince we have at most \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, we have \\(v\\le n-2\\) and therefore\n\n\\[\n\\text{non‑vertical lines}=n-v\\le n-(n-2)=2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **at most two** non‑vertical lines can exist, contradicting the necessity of three\nnon‑vertical lines derived above.\n\nThe contradiction shows that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe discussion yields\n\n* \\(k=0\\) – always possible (all vertical lines);\n* \\(k=1\\) – always possible (all vertical lines except the last column, plus one sunny line);\n* \\(k\\ge 2\\) – impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Verification of the constructions  \n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines used | Reason each required point is covered |\n|-------|------------|--------------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(\\ell_i:x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\) | For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\). |\n| 1 | \\(\\ell_i:x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) and \\(L:y-1=2(x-n)\\) | Points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) are on the corresponding vertical line. The unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) and satisfies \\(1-1=2(n-n)\\), so it lies on \\(L\\). |\n\nAll lines are distinct; in the first family each line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (non‑sunny);\nin the second family the added line has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and is therefore sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1.}\n\\]"
