at\nleast as many points of \\(T_n\\) as a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) or a diagonal\n\\(x+y=c\\) (both have at most the same number of points because the triangle is\nright‑angled).  \nThus we may suppose that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThe points covered by these lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-k}= \\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\n|C_{n-k}|=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-i)= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThe complement \\(T_n\\setminus C_{n-k}\\) consists of the points with\n\\(a>n-k\\).  Writing \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus C_{n-k}= \\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\}\n=:T_k .\n\\]\n\nHence the original problem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\)\n> sunny lines covering all points of the triangle \\(T_k\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge 2\\) and that \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) cover \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Border points.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nB_k=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(B_k\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=k+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\); therefore\nno two of them can belong to the same sunny line.  Consequently each\n\\(\\ell_i\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(B_k\\), and the \\(k\\) points of\n\\(B_k\\) are distributed one‑to‑one among the \\(k\\) lines.\n\n*Column argument.*  \nThe points of \\(B_k\\) occupy **all** columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) contains the border point \\((i,k+1-i)\\) and also another point\n\\((i,b)\\) with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, then \\(\\ell\\) would contain two points\nwith equal \\(x\\); the only line through two such points is the vertical line\n\\(x=i\\), which is forbidden.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) cannot contain any other point of\ncolumn \\(i\\).\n\nIn particular, column \\(x=1\\) already contains the border point \\((1,k)\\);\ntherefore **no** line can contain the point \\((1,1)\\) (which also lies in\ncolumn 1).  The point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to \\(T_k\\) for every \\(k\\ge 1\\), so it\nremains uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nThus a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible for every\n\\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and their union equals \\(T_n\\); hence a configuration with\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except the single point \\((n,1)\\)\n(because for \\(a=n\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\)).  \nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = x-(n-1) \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is sunny.\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) now lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly\none of them (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe reduction in §1 shows that the original question is equivalent to the\nreduced problem.  §2 proves that the reduced problem has **no** solution for\n\\(k\\ge 2\\); §3 provides explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\nConsequently the only possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
