ical).  \n   Therefore the column \\(i\\) contains **no** further point of the triangle that can be covered by this line.  \n   The column \\(i\\) still contains the interior points \\((i,1),\\dots,(i,k-i)\\); each of them must be covered by a *different* sunny line.  \n   Since there are \\(k\\) columns and only \\(k\\) sunny lines, the column \\(i=1\\) forces the line that contains the border point \\((1,k)\\) to be the *only* line that can meet column 1.  \n   Consequently the interior point \\((1,1)\\) cannot be covered – a contradiction.  \n   Hence a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible for every \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   All of them are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   These lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   y = x-(n-1) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1\\neq0,-1,\\infty),\n   \\]\n   which passes through \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Exactly one line is sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The reduction shows that the original problem is equivalent to covering the triangle \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Steps 3 and 4 prove that this is possible only for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n   Therefore the required set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
