            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\).  \nHence every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, so the required configuration exists for \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1)\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nVerification point‑by‑point:\n\n* For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) we have \\(x=a\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n* The only point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n  Substituting \\(a=n\\) into the equation of \\(S\\) gives \\(y=n-(n-1)=1\\); hence \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny, and the lines are distinct.  Thus a configuration exists for \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lemma on sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap T_n|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nFor an integer \\(x\\ge1\\) the corresponding \\(y\\) must satisfy the defining inequalities of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n1\\le mx+b\\le n+1-x .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand inequality of (1) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n(m+1)x\\le n+1-b .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\), the coefficient \\(m+1\\) is non‑zero.  Hence (2) can hold for at most one integer value of \\(x\\) when \\(m+1>0\\) (the left‑hand side grows with \\(x\\)), and for at most one integer value of \\(x\\) when \\(m+1<0\\) (the left‑hand side decreases with \\(x\\)).  \n\nThe left‑hand inequality of (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nmx\\ge 1-b\\Longrightarrow x\\ge\\frac{1-b}{m}\\quad\\text{if }m>0,\n\\]\n\\[\nmx\\le 1-b\\Longrightarrow x\\le\\frac{1-b}{m}\\quad\\text{if }m<0 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (2) these bounds restrict \\(x\\) to an interval of length strictly less than \\(2\\).  Consequently at most two integers satisfy (1), i.e. at most two points of \\(T_n\\) lie on \\(L\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(k\\ge2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy the lemma each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nIn each case the line meets the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the longest possible column, row or anti‑diagonal has exactly \\(n\\) points).  \nHence the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\n*Total coverage.*  \nAltogether at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+2k \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\) can be covered.  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+2k\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac12\\bigl(n^2-n\\bigr)-k\\bigl(n-2\\bigr)\\ge0 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-2\\ge1\\).  For any \\(k\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac12\\bigl(n^2-n\\bigr)-k\\bigl(n-2\\bigr)\n\\le\\frac12\\bigl(n^2-n\\bigr)-2\\bigl(n-2\\bigr)\n=\\frac12\\bigl(n^2-5n+4\\bigr)\n=\\frac12\\,(n-1)(n-4)<0 .\n\\]\n\nThus inequality (5) is violated, contradicting the assumption that a configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists.  Consequently no such configuration can exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe constructions of Section 1 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \nSection 3 proves that every \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.  \nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
