 are sunny.\n\nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\) (a direct inspection of all possible slopes shows that any line of slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) meeting the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_3)\\) can intersect the integer lattice in at most two points).\n\nThus the two sunny lines together cover at most four points, leaving at least two points to be covered by the remaining (non‑sunny) line.  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); each of these can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\).  \nA simple case‑by‑case check shows that no such line can contain the two *remaining* points simultaneously (the only pairs of points not lying on a common vertical, horizontal or \\(-1\\)‑slope line are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) – a forbidden slope).  \n\nHence a covering with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(T_4\\) consists of the ten points  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n&(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n&(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSuppose we have four distinct lines covering all ten points and at least two of them are sunny.\n\nA direct enumeration of all possible sunny lines intersecting the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_4)\\) shows that any such line contains at most three points of \\(T_4\\).  \nTherefore the two sunny lines together cover at most six points, leaving at least four points for the two non‑sunny lines.\n\nEach non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most four points of \\(T_4\\).  \nA short inspection of the possible placements of two non‑sunny lines shows that they cannot together cover the four *remaining* points without using a slope \\(-1\\) line that would also