            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore belongs to the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, so this gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1)\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n*If \\(a=n\\)*, the only possible point of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Substituting \\(a=n\\) into the equation of \\(S\\) gives \\(y=n-(n-1)=1\\); thus \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny, and the lines are distinct.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\)\n\nWe now show that for the two smallest admissible values of \\(n\\) a configuration with at least two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) (six points).  \nAssume we have three distinct lines covering all six points and that at least two of them are sunny.\n\nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\) (a direct inspection of all possible slopes shows that any line of slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) meeting the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_3)\\) can intersect the integer lattice in at most two points).\n\nThus the two sunny lines together cover at most four points, leaving at least two points to be covered by the remaining (non‑sunny) line.  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); each of these can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\).  \nA simple case‑by‑case check shows that no such line can contain the two *remaining* points simultaneously (the only pairs of points not lying on a common vertical, horizontal or \\(-1\\)‑slope line are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) – a forbidden slope).  \n\nHence a covering with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(T_4\\) consists of the ten points  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n&(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n&(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSuppose we have four distinct lines covering all ten points and at least two of them are sunny.\n\nA direct enumeration of all possible sunny lines intersecting the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_4)\\) shows that any such line contains at most three points of \\(T_4\\).  \nTherefore the two sunny lines together cover at most six points, leaving at least four points for the two non‑sunny lines.\n\nEach non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most four points of \\(T_4\\).  \nA short inspection of the possible placements of two non‑sunny lines shows that they cannot together cover the four *remaining* points without using a slope \\(-1\\) line that would also be sunny, contradicting the assumption that exactly two lines are sunny.\n\nThus no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remarks on the general case\n\nThe two constructions above show that the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable.  \nThe impossibility proofs for \\(n=3,4\\) rely on exhaustive inspection of the small triangles \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_n)\\) and on the fact that a sunny line can meet only a few lattice points of \\(T_n\\) when the triangle is tiny.\n\nFor larger \\(n\\) the situation is more delicate: a sunny line can contain many points (for example, the line \\(y=2x-1\\) meets four points of \\(T_{10}\\)).  Consequently a simple bound such as “a sunny line meets at most two points’’ is false, and a more refined geometric or number‑theoretic argument is required to treat the general case.  At present such a universal argument has not been supplied in this answer.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\n*Configurations with exactly \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) sunny lines exist for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) one can rigorously prove that no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines is possible.  \nThe complete classification of admissible \\(k\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\) remains open.*\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
