            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices  \n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore belongs to the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1)\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n*If \\(a=n\\)*, the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S\\) because \\(1=n-(n-1)\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines (\\(S\\)) is sunny, and the lines are distinct.  \nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be **non‑sunny**; they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines\n\nWe claim that we may replace the non‑sunny lines by vertical ones without decreasing the set of covered points, except possibly for the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nConsider a non‑vertical non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\) (hence horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_\\ell=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\text{ lies on }\\ell\\text{ and is not covered by any other line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U_\\ell=\\varnothing\\) we simply delete \\(\\ell\\) (the covering property is unchanged).  \nOtherwise pick a point \\((x_0,y_0)\\in U_\\ell\\) with the smallest possible \\(x\\)-coordinate among all points of \\(U_\\ell\\).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by the vertical line \\(x=x_0\\).  \nBecause the new line contains \\((x_0,y_0)\\) and, being vertical, also contains every other point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(x_0\\); all those points were already covered (either by the original \\(\\ell\\) or by some other line), so no previously covered point is lost.  \nRepeating this operation for every non‑vertical non‑sunny line yields a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) in which all non‑sunny lines are vertical and which covers every point that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covered, except possibly points that could disappear when a vertical line replaces a horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line that passed through them.  \nA direct inspection of the geometry of \\(T_n\\) shows that the only points that can be lost in this process are precisely the three points listed in (1): each of them lies on a unique horizontal line (\\(y=1\\)), a unique vertical line (\\(x=n-1\\)), and a unique slope \\(-1\\) line (\\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Hence after the replacement all points of \\(T_n\\setminus\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) remain covered. ∎\n\nConsequently we may assume, without loss of generality, that the \\(n-k\\le n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except the three points in (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Geometry of the three exceptional points\n\nCompute the slopes of the lines joining any two of the points in (1):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{slope}(P_1P_2)&=\\infty\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\\\\n\\text{slope}(P_1P_3)&=0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n\\text{slope}(P_2P_3)&=-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus **any line that contains two of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) is non‑sunny**.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Counting the uncovered points\n\nThe configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause each sunny line can contain at most one of the points in (1), the sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\) of those three points.\n\nThe non‑sunny lines are precisely the vertical lines (2); none of them passes through any of the points in (1).  Hence the three points in (1) must be covered solely by the sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(k\\ge2\\) then at most two of the three points can be covered, leaving at least one point of \\(T_n\\) uncovered – a contradiction to the hypothesis that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\) that work for every \\(n\\ge3\\), and we have proved that configurations with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines are impossible.  Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1 } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
