"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the previous answer.  \nThe complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }3\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nIn other words, \\(k=2\\) is impossible, while all other non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\).  \n   For each construction a point\u2011by\u2011point verification is given in the detailed part.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The three points  \n   \\[\n   P_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1)\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   belong to \\(T_n\\).  \n   Any line that contains two of them has slope \\(\\in\\{\\,\\infty,0,-1\\,\\}\\) and is therefore **non\u2011sunny**.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n\n   With exactly two sunny lines we have at most two of the points in (1) covered by sunny lines.  \n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain **at most two** of the points in (1), and the only ways to do so are  \n   \\[\n   x=n-1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Since we have only \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, at most one of the three lines in (2) can be used, so at most two of the points in (1) are covered by non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Altogether we can cover at most \\(2\\) (sunny)\u202f\\(+\\)\u202f\\(2\\) (non\u2011sunny)\u202