((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \n  - Slope \\(-2\\) is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n- **Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n### Step 5 – Three‑sunny‑line covering of the last three columns\n> Definition of lines:  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> S_1 &: \\;x+2y=n+2,\\\\\n> S_2 &: \\;2x+y=2n-1,\\\\\n> S_3 &: \\;x-y=n-3 .\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]  \n> “The three lines are distinct and have slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,-2,\\;1\\)… The six points  \n> \\((n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\) and each of them lies on one of the lines in (1).”\n\n- **Verification of points:** Direct substitution (shown in analysis) confirms each point lies on the indicated line. All six points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n> “Consequently the family  \n> \\(\\mathcal C_n=\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3,\\,S_1,\\,S_2,\\,S_3\\,\\}\\)  \n> consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n- **Verification:**  \n  - The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-3\\).  \n  - The six points listed cover the remaining points in columns \\(n-2,n-1,n\\).  \n  - No other points remain uncovered.  \n- **Conclusion:** Correct; the construction yields a valid configuration with \\(k=3\\).\n\n### Step 6 – Increasing the number of sunny lines\n> “Starting from the family \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) of step 5, replace any of the vertical lines \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-3)\\) by a sunny line that passes through the same point \\((i,1)\\) and has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for instance \\(y=-2x+(2i+1)\\)).  \n> The point \\((i,1)\\) remains covered, and every other point of the column \\(x=i\\) is already covered by one of the three lines \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\) (because each of those three lines meets the column \\(x=i\\) at a point with \\(y\\ge1\\)).  \n> Consequently the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines is increased by one.  \n> Repeating this replacement we can turn any subset of the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines into sunny lines. Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\).”\n\n- **Verification of the claim:**  \n  - The new sunny line through \\((i,1)\\) covers only that single point of column \\(i\\).  \n  - The remaining points of column \\(i\\) are \\((i,b)\\) for \\(b=2,\\dots ,n+1-i\\).  \n  - The three lines \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\) intersect column \\(i\\) at the following (possibly non‑integer) \\(y\\)-coordinates:  \n    - \\(S_1:\\; y = \\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\) (may be non‑integer; for many \\(i\\) it is not an integer, so no integer point of the column lies on \\(S_1\\)).  \n    - \\(S_2:\\; y = 2n-1-2i\\) (always integer but typically much larger than the required range; for \\(i\\le n-3\\) this gives \\(y\\ge5\\)).  \n    - \\(S_3:\\; y = i-n+3\\le0\\) (never a positive integer).  \n  - Hence **most** points \\((i,2), (i,3),\\dots\\) are **not** covered by any of \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\).  \n  - Example: take \\(i=n-3\\). Then the column contains the points \\((n-3,2)\\) and \\((n-3,3)\\).  \n    - \\(S_1\\) gives \\(y=(n+2-(n-3))/2 = 5/2\\) (non‑integer).  \n    - \\(S_2\\) gives \\(y=2n-1-2(n-3)=5\\).  \n    - \\(S_3\\) gives \\(y=0\\).  \n    - Neither \\((n-3,2)\\) nor \\((n-3,3)\\) lies on any of these three lines.  \n  - Therefore after replacing the vertical line \\(x=i\\) the column \\(i\\) is **no longer fully covered**.  \n- **Conclusion:** The argument that the covering property is preserved is false. This step introduces a **Critical Error**, invalidating the claim that configurations exist for all \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n### Step 7 – Conclusion\n> “Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }3\\le k\\le n\\).”\n\n- **Verification:** Because Step 6 is incorrect, the existence of configurations for \\(k>3\\)